---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import WaitlistForm from "../components/WaitlistForm.astro";
---
<Base title="Download" description="KonaClaw for macOS on Apple silicon. Free trial at launch, then a paid license. Leave an email to be told when it ships.">
  <section class="pt-16">
    <h1 class="text-4xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-text-strong">Get KonaClaw</h1>
    <p class="mt-4 max-w-prose text-lg text-text">The first release is being signed and notarized now. Leave an email and you will get exactly one message the day it is live.</p>
    <div class="mt-8"><WaitlistForm /></div>
  </section>
  <section class="prose-kc mt-16 grid gap-10 md:grid-cols-2">
    <div>
      <h2>How it will work</h2>
      <p>Download a disk image, drag KonaClaw to Applications, open it. A short setup asks for the permissions you want to grant and downloads a model sized to your Mac. First chat in a few minutes.</p>
      <p><strong>Free trial, then a paid license.</strong> Try everything first. Pricing is announced at launch.</p>
    </div>
    <div>
      <h2>Requirements</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>A Mac with Apple silicon (M1 or later).</li>
        <li>macOS 14 (Sonoma) or later.</li>
        <li>16 GB of memory or more. Larger models want 32 GB or more.</li>
        <li>About 10 GB of disk for the app and a first model.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>The rule of thumb: a 4-bit model needs roughly 0.7&nbsp;GB per billion parameters, plus room for macOS and your apps. KonaClaw checks your Mac at first launch and never downloads a model that doesn't fit comfortably.</p>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="mt-16">
    <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-text-strong">A model sized to your Mac</h2>
    <p class="mt-3 max-w-prose text-text">Setup picks the sweet-spot model for your machine automatically, and you can switch any time in Settings. Here is what each tier runs, with approximate generation speeds for 4-bit MLX builds in a chat workload (long contexts and thermal throttling pull these down a little).</p>
    <div class="mt-6 overflow-x-auto">
      <table class="spec-table">
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th scope="col">Your Mac</th>
            <th scope="col">Recommended MLX models</th>
            <th scope="col">Approx. speed</th>
            <th scope="col">What it feels like</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row" class="whitespace-nowrap">M1 · 16 GB<br class="hidden sm:block"><span class="text-muted-aa text-sm font-normal">(Air, mini, MBP)</span></th>
            <td>Qwen3&nbsp;8B · Llama&nbsp;3.1&nbsp;8B</td>
            <td class="whitespace-nowrap">~15–20 tok/s</td>
            <td>Comfortable chat, drafts and mail triage. Stick to 8B and close the 40-tab browser window.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row" class="whitespace-nowrap">M1 Pro / Max · M2 · M3<br><span class="text-muted-aa text-sm font-normal">16–32 GB</span></th>
            <td>Qwen3&nbsp;14B · gpt-oss-20b<br><span class="text-muted-aa text-sm">24B if you have 32&nbsp;GB</span></td>
            <td class="whitespace-nowrap">~25–45 tok/s</td>
            <td>The capable middle of the road. 32&nbsp;GB unlocks Mistral&nbsp;Small&nbsp;24B and its much better tool use.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row" class="whitespace-nowrap">M4 · M5<br><span class="text-muted-aa text-sm font-normal">24–32 GB</span></th>
            <td>Mistral&nbsp;Small&nbsp;24B · Qwen3-30B-A3B</td>
            <td class="whitespace-nowrap">~60–90 tok/s</td>
            <td>Fast. The sparse MoE models (only ~3B active parameters) answer faster than you read.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row" class="whitespace-nowrap">M4 Pro / Max · M5 Pro / Max<br><span class="text-muted-aa text-sm font-normal">48–64 GB</span></th>
            <td>Qwen3&nbsp;32B · gpt-oss-120b<br><span class="text-muted-aa text-sm">120B needs 64&nbsp;GB</span></td>
            <td class="whitespace-nowrap">~35–55 tok/s at 120B</td>
            <td>Frontier-class quality, still local. gpt-oss-120b is the best model that fits a desk Mac today.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row" class="whitespace-nowrap">Studio / Ultra · M3 Ultra and later<br><span class="text-muted-aa text-sm font-normal">96–512 GB</span></th>
            <td>Llama&nbsp;3.3&nbsp;70B · gpt-oss-120b · bigger still</td>
            <td class="whitespace-nowrap">~30–50 tok/s at 70B+</td>
            <td>Server-class models on your desk, quietly. Everything the catalog offers, at full size.</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="mt-4 text-sm text-muted-aa">Speeds are ballpark figures from public MLX benchmarks on comparable hardware, not promises; your mileage varies with context length, background load, and whether the fans have anything better to do. The first-launch benchmark measures your actual Mac and labels each model honestly before you download it.</p>
  </section>
  <section class="mt-16">
    <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-text-strong">Qwen&nbsp;27B at 4-bit: M1 through M6</h2>
    <p class="mt-3 max-w-prose text-text">One model, every generation of Apple silicon. Generation speed is memory-bandwidth-bound, so the bars track the pipe, not the launch year. Prefill (reading your prompt, documents and code) is compute-bound and shown separately &mdash; it is the column old Macs lose hardest. The 4-bit Qwen&nbsp;27B weighs about 15&nbsp;GB, so the fit column tells you whether it can even move into your Mac&rsquo;s unified memory.</p>
