    </div>
    <p class="mt-4 text-sm text-muted-aa"><strong>Reading the table:</strong> the base model is Qwen&nbsp;27B at 4-bit MLX quantization (~15&nbsp;GB of weights + ~1.3&nbsp;GB of KV cache per 4&thinsp;K tokens of context). Generation speed is <em>bandwidth&nbsp;&divide;&nbsp;15&nbsp;GB</em> in the limit, which is why an M1&nbsp;Max outruns a new base&nbsp;M4 and why &ldquo;2023 laptop&rdquo; tells you nothing on its own. Prefill is compute-bound &mdash; the M1&rsquo;s original GPU shuffles a 32&thinsp;K-token document in about 4 minutes where an M4&nbsp;Max needs ~40&nbsp;seconds. <strong>M1 owners:</strong> at 16&nbsp;GB the 27B genuinely does not fit &mdash; and at 8&nbsp;GB nothing bigger than 7B does. KonaClaw offers you Qwen3&nbsp;8B instead, which runs respectably. <strong>M5&nbsp;Pro, M5&nbsp;Ultra and M6 are projections</strong> from public analyst forecasts and architectural trends, marked <span class="proj-tag">est</span> &mdash; we will publish measured numbers the week each machine ships, measured by KonaClaw&rsquo;s first-launch benchmark on your exact silicon.</p>
  </section>
</Base>
